{
  "id": "2026-10-12-watching-the-watchers",
  "title": "A coder built a camera to track the cops, and the cops knocked on his door",
  "topic": "테크",
  "level": 3,
  "voice": "en-US-AndrewMultilingualNeural",
  "sentences": [
    "Good morning!",
    "A Canadian software engineer named Anthony Sistilli built something that turned heads this week.",
    "Using the same kind of technology that companies like Flock sell to police, he made a homemade camera that reads license plates automatically.",
    "The twist is that his version only tracks police cars.",
    "He posted videos explaining how it works, and they quickly went viral.",
    "Not long after, two officers showed up at his front door and asked about his intentions for the data.",
    "One of them warned that in the wrong hands, this kind of data could reveal where officers live and when they work.",
    "Sistilli's point was that the same risk already exists when the cameras are pointed at ordinary people.",
    "His town had just spent two million dollars on this exact technology, with little public debate about how long the data is kept.",
    "It's a sharp reminder that surveillance tools cut both ways.",
    "What do you think — should cities be more transparent about the cameras they buy?"
  ],
  "summary_ko": [
    "캐나다 개발자가 경찰차만 추적하는 자작 번호판 인식 카메라를 만들어 영상으로 공개했다.",
    "곧 경찰이 집에 찾아와 수집한 데이터의 의도를 물었고, 잘못 쓰이면 위험하다고 경고했다.",
    "같은 감시 기술이 시민을 향할 때의 위험은 왜 안 따지냐는 문제 제기 — 감시는 양날의 칼이다."
  ],
